# sudoers rule for tgproxy-panel (plan.md §7 "Права доступа").
#
# Install to: /etc/sudoers.d/tgproxy-panel
# Mode:       0440
# Owner:      root:root
#
# Before trusting this file, validate its syntax with:
#   visudo -c -f /etc/sudoers.d/tgproxy-panel
# (deploy/install.sh, a later stage, is expected to do this automatically
# as part of installing it — never load an unvalidated sudoers fragment.)
#
# Grants the unprivileged `tgproxy-panel` system user NOPASSWD permission
# to run exactly one script, apply-profiles.sh, as root — and only with an
# argument that is a candidate file under
# /opt/tgproxy-panel/backup/candidates/. This is deliberately narrow: no
# other command, no arbitrary argument, no shell metacharacters (sudoers
# command-line matching here is literal argument matching, not a shell
# glob that would expand `*`, `..`, etc. — see `man 5 sudoers`,
# "Command execution" / "Wildcards" sections). sudoers glob syntax
# (fnmatch(3)-based) only supports simple `*`/`?`/`[...]` wildcards, not
# `**` or full shell globbing; a single trailing `*` is sufficient here
# because internal/applier/candidate.go always writes candidates as flat
# files directly inside that one directory, never in a subdirectory.
#
# KNOWN CONSTRAINT: the path below is hardcoded to the default
# BACKUP_DIR=/opt/tgproxy-panel/backup (see internal/config.Config.BackupDir
# and .env.example). If a deployment's .env ever sets a non-default
# BACKUP_DIR, this file no longer matches where internal/applier actually
# stages candidates, and every apply will fail at the sudo step ("sorry,
# you are not allowed"). deploy/install.sh (a later stage) must either:
#   (a) keep BACKUP_DIR=/opt/tgproxy-panel/backup as a fixed,
#       non-configurable installation convention, or
#   (b) regenerate this file from the actual configured BACKUP_DIR at
#       install time instead of copying it verbatim.
# This file does not enforce which option install.sh takes — it only
# documents that the coupling exists and must be honored by whichever
# stage writes the final /etc/sudoers.d/tgproxy-panel.
#
# apply-profiles.sh itself re-validates that its argument resolves inside
# this same directory (defense in depth — see its own header comment), so
# a sudoers misconfiguration alone cannot turn this into an arbitrary-file
# installer, but it *can* break applies outright if BACKUP_DIR diverges.

tgproxy-panel ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /opt/tgproxy-panel/bin/apply-profiles.sh /opt/tgproxy-panel/backup/candidates/*
